WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.652
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B one.

2
00:00:05.952 --> 00:00:09.420
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.845
Unit one: Present Perfect. Today you will link past experiences to the present.

4
00:00:15.145 --> 00:00:17.821
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:22.127
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.983 --> 00:00:23.903
Have done.

7
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:28.658
Have done means the present perfect form for I, you, we and they.

8
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:30.704
I have done my homework.

9
00:00:31.317 --> 00:00:33.783
Your turn. Say: Have done.

10
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:37.542
Have done.

11
00:00:38.283 --> 00:00:39.284
Has done.

12
00:00:40.283 --> 00:00:43.897
Has done means the present perfect form for he, she and it.

13
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:46.030
She has done her best.

14
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:49.304
Your turn. Say: Has done.

15
00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:53.098
Has done.

16
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:54.712
Already.

17
00:00:55.717 --> 00:00:58.564
Already means before now or sooner than expected.

18
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:00.534
I have already eaten.

19
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.756
Your turn. Say: Already.

20
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.798
Already.

21
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.516
Yet.

22
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:13.689
Yet means up to now, used in questions and negatives.

23
00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:15.656
Have you finished yet?

24
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.415
Your turn. Say: Yet.

25
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:22.094
Yet.

26
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:23.607
Ever.

27
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:26.825
Ever means at any time in your life.

28
00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:28.882
Have you ever been abroad?

29
00:01:29.483 --> 00:01:31.845
Your turn. Say: Ever.

30
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:35.504
Ever.

31
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:37.042
Never.

32
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.701
Never means at no time.

33
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:41.933
I have never seen snow.

34
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:44.867
Your turn. Say: Never.

35
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:48.305
Never.

36
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.318
Experience.

37
00:01:51.317 --> 00:01:54.558
Experience means something you have done or lived through.

38
00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.010
Travel is a great experience.

39
00:01:57.617 --> 00:02:00.490
Your turn. Say: Experience.

40
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:04.404
Experience.

41
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.035
Since.

42
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.839
Since means from a point in the past until now.

43
00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.926
I have lived here since 2020.

44
00:02:13.517 --> 00:02:16.006
Your turn. Say: Since.

45
00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:19.821
Since.

46
00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:24.563
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:25.417 --> 00:02:30.019
Visal, have you ever travelled to another country? Answer with the present perfect.

48
00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:40.064
Yes, I have been to Thailand. We form the present perfect with have or has and the past participle. It links past experiences to the present.

49
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.405
When do we use it instead of the past simple?

50
00:02:44.517 --> 00:02:55.830
When the time is not named or is not finished: I have visited Thailand. If we say when, we use the past simple: I visited Thailand in 2022.

51
00:02:56.917 --> 00:02:59.125
What about already, yet and since?

52
00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:14.653
Already is for things done earlier than expected, yet is for questions and negatives, and since gives a starting point: I have lived here since 2020. Ever and never talk about life experience.

53
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.579
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:18.417 --> 00:03:23.574
Grammar focus: Present perfect: have and has plus past participle.

55
00:03:23.874 --> 00:03:25.003
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:25.717 --> 00:03:27.577
I have been to Thailand twice.

57
00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:30.355
She has already finished her homework.

58
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:32.878
Have you ever eaten durian?

59
00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:41.826
Use have / has + past participle for experiences and past actions with a present link. Do not use it with a finished time such as yesterday.

60
00:03:42.583 --> 00:03:46.234
Be careful! Don't say: I have seen him yesterday.

61
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.269
Say: I saw him yesterday.

62
00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:52.871
Use the past simple with yesterday.

63
00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:56.261
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:58.660
Life Experiences.

65
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.341
I have visited several countries, but I have never seen snow.

66
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:05.820
My sister has already finished her homework.

67
00:04:06.833 --> 00:04:10.519
Have you ever tried durian? It is an unusual experience.

68
00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:13.034
They haven't arrived yet.

69
00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:16.997
I have lived in this town since 2018.

70
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:22.415
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.203
I blank to Japan twice.

72
00:04:25.503 --> 00:04:28.692
A, have been. B, was. C, am been.

73
00:04:32.233 --> 00:04:34.244
The answer is A: have been.

74
00:04:35.383 --> 00:04:37.011
She blank finished yet.

75
00:04:37.311 --> 00:04:41.011
A, hasn't. B, didn't. C, doesn't.

76
00:04:44.567 --> 00:04:46.671
The answer is A: hasn't.

77
00:04:47.817 --> 00:04:50.153
Which time word needs the past simple?

78
00:04:50.453 --> 00:04:54.130
A, ever. B, yesterday. C, already.

79
00:04:57.667 --> 00:04:59.724
The answer is B: yesterday.

80
00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:02.593
Which sentence is correct?

81
00:05:02.893 --> 00:05:08.206
A, I have seen him yesterday.. B, I have seen him before.. C, I have saw him before..

82
00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:14.869
The answer is B: I have seen him before..

83
00:05:16.017 --> 00:05:19.061
According to the reading, since when has the writer lived in town?

84
00:05:19.361 --> 00:05:23.758
A, 2018. B, yesterday. C, last week.

85
00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.217
The answer is A: 2018.

86
00:05:31.433 --> 00:05:34.492
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:39.790
Have you ever traveled to another country? Answer using the present perfect.

88
00:05:48.583 --> 00:05:50.362
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:50.662 --> 00:06:06.413
Yes, I have travelled to another country. I have been to Thailand twice, and I have enjoyed every visit. I use the present perfect because the time is not important, and the experience is part of my life now.

90
00:06:06.713 --> 00:06:08.365
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:15.165
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present perfect: have and has plus past participle.

92
00:06:15.465 --> 00:06:19.348
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

93
00:06:19.648 --> 00:06:22.079
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

94
00:06:22.379 --> 00:06:25.334
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

